WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.333
System.

2
00:00:01.333 --> 00:00:02.666
Text.

3
00:00:02.666 --> 00:00:06.664
Json is a common framework-supported serializer for modern .

4
00:00:06.664 --> 00:00:07.997
NET applications.

5
00:00:07.997 --> 00:00:09.329
It is used for ASP.

6
00:00:09.329 --> 00:00:18.592
NET Core APIs, background services, message processing, import and export flows, configuration-like data, and service-to-service contracts.

7
00:00:18.592 --> 00:00:25.722
Its presence in the platform is helpful because teams can rely on maintained behavior and familiar options.

8
00:00:25.722 --> 00:00:30.920
But safe use still depends on how the application shapes data at the boundary.

9
00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.386
DTOs should be intentional contracts.

10
00:00:33.386 --> 00:00:42.248
An ExampleCustomerDto, ExampleOrderMessage, or ExampleApiResponse should contain the fields that belong at that boundary and no more.

11
00:00:42.248 --> 00:00:55.709
Directly serializing broad domain objects can expose internal state, lifecycle flags, authorization-related fields, tenant markers, notes, or implementation details that were never meant for the caller.

12
00:00:55.709 --> 00:01:02.706
Directly deserializing broad domain objects can also populate properties the operation should not accept.

13
00:01:02.706 --> 00:01:07.304
After JSON deserialization, server-side validation is still required.

14
00:01:07.304 --> 00:01:10.769
The serializer can create an object with the right .

15
00:01:10.769 --> 00:01:26.296
NET type, but it does not know whether the ExampleTenantId belongs to the current user, whether a field is allowed for this workflow, whether a date range is business-valid, or whether an optional field is acceptable in this context.

16
00:01:26.296 --> 00:01:34.626
Validation should happen before business decisions, authorization-sensitive decisions, persistence, or downstream processing.

17
00:01:34.626 --> 00:01:43.156
Required fields, optional fields, defaults, unknown properties, naming policies, and type conversion assumptions deserve review.

18
00:01:43.156 --> 00:01:49.353
A missing value might be acceptable in one version of a contract and unacceptable in another.

19
00:01:49.353 --> 00:01:54.084
A default value might be safe for display but unsafe for state changes.

20
00:01:54.084 --> 00:02:02.014
Unknown fields may be ignored by design, but teams should understand what that means for compatibility and diagnostics.

21
00:02:02.014 --> 00:02:04.280
Avoid overbroad object population.

22
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:12.676
An update endpoint should not accept every property that exists on the server model just because JSON binding can populate it.

23
00:02:12.676 --> 00:02:25.737
For example, fields that affect ownership, approval state, price, role, tenant, or administrative behavior should usually be assigned by server-side logic, not accepted from a general request DTO.

24
00:02:25.737 --> 00:02:28.269
Use narrow DTOs for narrow operations.

25
00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:32.068
Custom converters are powerful and deserve higher review.

26
00:02:32.068 --> 00:02:38.065
A converter can change how values are read, written, normalized, interpreted, or rejected.

27
00:02:38.065 --> 00:02:42.597
It can also create behavior that is not obvious from the DTO itself.

28
00:02:42.597 --> 00:02:53.525
Review custom converters for scope, error handling, compatibility, validation assumptions, and whether they preserve the boundary contract rather than undermine it.
